module sysl.posix.tty
@requires(posix, libc)

import sysl.tty as modes

/** Getting the kernel out of the way, so a program can read a terminal a keystroke at a time.
 *
 * **This is what makes one program run at a macOS terminal and over a serial cable to a board.** At a
 * shell the line discipline echoes what is typed, honours backspace, and releases a whole line at
 * Enter -- so `sysl.term.edit` would echo a second copy of every character and never see a keystroke
 * until it was too late to act on one. Over a USB CDC port there is no line discipline to begin with,
 * which is why the editor exists. These functions are the difference between those situations, and
 * they are the *only* difference: with them the same program serves both.
 *
 * A program that wants whole lines and no editing wants none of this. `sysl.io.console_lines` reads a
 * cooked terminal, the kernel does the work, and that is the right facility for anything that is not
 * drawing its own line.
 *
 * ## They are `sysl.tty`'s, under the names this module has always had
 *
 * `raw`, `hidden` and `cooked` are `sysl.tty.raw`, `sysl.tty.hidden` and `sysl.tty.cooked`, which go
 * through the hooks of `sysl.tty.sys` -- answered over `termios` on a host, and by a kernel's own
 * `@export`s where there is no C library. That module carries the argument: what each state sets and
 * why, why signals go, and how the terminal is put back at exit. A program that only ever runs on a
 * host may keep importing them from here; one that should also run where a kernel owns the terminal
 * imports `sysl.tty`, which asks for no C library.
 */

// `fflush` with no stream, for `flush` below. Declared here rather than in `sysl.sys`, following
// `sysl.fs`: `sysl.sys` is the printing-and-reading core, carries no capability, and a C-library
// symbol in it would be a requirement filed where nothing declares one.
private[sysl] extern "fflush" c_fflush(stream: *u8) -> int

// `fflush(stdout)`, which cannot be written here. See the sink at the foot of this file, and the C
// beside it for why the stream has to be named on that side.
private[sysl] extern "sysl_tty_flush_out" c_flush_out() -> unit

/** Put the terminal into cbreak mode: keystrokes arrive as they are typed and nothing is echoed --
 * `sysl.tty.raw`. **Answers whether it worked**; it fails where there is no terminal to change.
 *
 *     if raw()
 *         var ed = editor(&input, &output)
 *         for line in ed do ...
 *         cooked()
 */
raw() -> bool = modes.raw()

/** Put the terminal into password mode: whole lines, nothing echoed -- `sysl.tty.hidden`. */
hidden() -> bool = modes.hidden()

/** Put back what `raw` or `hidden` changed, and only what they changed -- `sysl.tty.cooked`. */
cooked() = modes.cooked()

// ## Getting output out, which a terminal needs and a pipe does not
//
// **`print` does not reach the screen when you expect it to, and that breaks every prompt.**
// `sysl.putbytes` -- the one sink all printing goes through -- writes with C's `fwrite`, and C
// line-buffers a stream attached to a terminal. So a prompt written without a newline sits in the
// buffer, and so does every character an editor echoes, until something writes a `\n`. What a person
// sees is a program that prints nothing while they type and then produces the whole line at once,
// which reads as an editor that is not echoing at all.
//
// A board never shows this, which is why it was found here and not there: its `putchar` is the SDK's
// and has no buffer in front of it.
//
// **The obvious fix is `setvbuf(stdout, …)` once, and it is refused for a reason that is ISO C's
// rather than sysl's.** `setvbuf` may be called only *before any other operation* on the stream, and
// a library function reached through `raw()` cannot promise that: a program that prints a prompt and
// then enters cbreak mode has already written to standard output. It happens to work on both libcs
// here, which is what makes it the dangerous kind of wrong.
//
// **So the buffering stays and the flush is what is made cheap.** What was written here was
// `fflush(null)` -- *every* open stream, on every keystroke, to push the one the terminal reads --
// and what is written now names the stream. It names it in **C**, one line beside this file, because
// `stdout` is a `#define` on Darwin (`__stdoutp`) and a symbol under glibc: an `extern` variable
// reaches it under one spelling and not the other. `reference/ffi.md § An extern also declares a
// variable` is the form and it is real -- it reaches `environ` and `optind` -- but a macro is one of
// the three things only C can reach, and on this platform `stdout` is one.

/** Push out everything the C library is holding, on every stream.
 *
 * **A caller needs this for its own prompt.** The editor's sink below flushes what the *editor*
 * writes; a prompt printed by the program before handing over is the program's own output, and
 * nothing has written a newline after it. `prints("ogol> ")` then `flush()` is the whole idiom.
 */
flush() = c_fflush(null)

/** Standard output as a sink that **flushes what it is given**, which is what an editor must write
 * through on a hosted terminal.
 *
 * It carries no state for the reason `Stdout` does not: a destination fixed at compile time keeps
 * none, so the pointer the sink is built on addresses nothing and `write` never looks at `self`.
 *
 * A program on a board passes its own port's writer and needs none of this. That asymmetry is real
 * and is the only one in the whole facility: the board has no buffer to defeat.
 */
struct TtyWriter
end TtyWriter

impl Fallible for TtyWriter

impl Writer for TtyWriter
    write(*self, bytes: []const u8)
        putbytes(bytes)
        c_flush_out()

val tty_sink: *TtyWriter = ptr_cast(0usize)

tty_writer() -> *Writer = tty_sink
